{#
  The provider fabric's L2 domain: a second libvirt network alongside the
  lab's existing 'default' (virbr0).

  Deliberately minimal, and every omission is load-bearing:

  <forward mode='open'/> - libvirt creates and manages the bridge but adds NO
      firewall rules of its own for it. That is the whole reason this is
      'open' and not an isolated network (no <forward> element at all, or
      mode='isolated'): an isolated network gets

        -A FORWARD -i virbr1 -j REJECT
        -A FORWARD -o virbr1 -j REJECT

      which is fine while traffic is only ever switched inside the bridge -
      until something loads br_netfilter and sets bridge-nf-call-iptables=1,
      at which point bridged frames start traversing FORWARD too and the
      first of those rules silently kills the entire fabric. Docker loads
      br_netfilter, and clab_deploy_mode: host puts Docker on this very host.
      'open' sidesteps the whole class of problem.

      Isolation is not lost by this: there is no <ip> below, so the host has
      no address on the fabric and no route into it, and there is no <forward>
      NAT to anywhere. Nothing can leave virbr1 because nothing is routing
      for it - not because a REJECT rule says so.

  no <ip>, no <dhcp>, no <dns> - every address on this network is static,
      configured by NMState on the OCP nodes and by the containerlab FRR
      configs on the fabric side. A dnsmasq here would be a second DHCP
      server on a bridge the OCP nodes are attached to, which is exactly the
      sort of thing that ruins an afternoon. Leaving the host itself off the
      fabric also keeps the "provider network" genuinely external, which is
      the point of the simulation.

  MTU {{ clab_fabric_mtu }} - the fabric carries Geneve (phases 1-3) or VXLAN
      (phase 4) wrapped around pod traffic that is itself already at the
      cluster MTU. At 1500 the BGP sessions come up fine and then large flows
      black-hole, which is a miserable thing to debug. libvirt applies this to
      the bridge and to every tap it creates on it; the guests still need
      their own MTU set, which the NMState policies and the containerlab
      topology both do.

  STP stays on (libvirt's default) - harmless here, and the phase-4 EVPN
  fabric puts more than one device on this bridge.
#}
<network>
  <name>{{ clab_fabric_network }}</name>
  <bridge name='{{ clab_fabric_bridge }}' stp='on' delay='0'/>
  <mtu size='{{ clab_fabric_mtu }}'/>
  <forward mode='open'/>
</network>
